{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16120 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "Mono-O-Demethylcurcumin is implicated in the target pathway leading to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a series of molecular interactions involving macrophage scavenger receptors. The evidence suggests that Mono-O-Demethylcurcumin acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 6-Methoxykaempferol-3-O-Alpha-L-Rhamnopyranosyl-(1->6)-Beta-D-Glucopyranoside and [3-(3-Hydroxy-4,5-dimethoxyphenyl)-3-oxopropyl] acetate, which in turn target the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This indicates a potential mechanism where Mono-O-Demethylcurcumin may interact with these compounds, which then exert their effects on the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The shared involvement of macrophage scavenger receptors in the target pathways suggests a possible role for Mono-O-Demethylcurcumin in modulating the activity of these complexes, potentially through receptor-mediated signaling or direct interaction. The integration of these pathways highlights the complex network of molecular interactions that may underlie the biological effects of Mono-O-Demethylcurcumin.",
